+
+Caller-backed application allocation children: source-review stage
+=================================================================
+
+This fail-stop revision is UNCOMPILED and its new controls are UNEXECUTED.
+The previous fe88 process-inventory revision has a separately frozen build
+and component qualification packet.  Its overall ABI check FAILED, exit 2
+(19 of 20 checks passed); no compatibility exception was accepted.  Those
+artifacts do not qualify this changed allocation/attachment implementation.
+This revision provides
+an additive storage component, not an accepting QEMU/Source/RR/application
+profile.  Previous dae19 pthread results, installed artifacts and ABI results
+qualify that previous revision only.  They do not qualify this new libc/loader
+pair, its changed thread descriptor, its global footprint or these symbols.
+No caller may infer a containing parent or semantic operation permission from
+libc initialization, a pointer, thread id, copied receipt or allocation count.
+
+The native caller supplies separately admitted, exclusively retained actual
+application backing under its real process parent.  The application child is
+distinct from pthread metadata, supplied-stack storage and their custody.
+libc cannot authenticate a QEMU/Source parent.  The caller retains its real
+parent binding and all original debit/backing/post histories before effects.
+
+Physical layout and finite bounds
+--------------------------------
+
+MALLOC_BOUNDED_VERSION 1 adds malloc-bounded.h and twelve GLIBC_2.39 symbols:
+layout, init, query, allocate, allocation, reallocate, release, attach,
+attachment, detach, bind and tryretire, all with suffix _np.  Existing ordinary
+unattached entry points retain their behavior.  Only Linux/x86-64 LP64 init
+is supported; only that ABI list is extended.  No other architecture is qualified.
+
+The actual arena contains, in order:
+  sizeof (struct __malloc_bounded)
+  requests * sizeof (struct allocation)
+  attachments * sizeof (struct attachment)
+  monotonically allocated payload and actual alignment padding.
+
+Initialization checks every addition/multiplication and reserves
+requests * (max (request_bytes, 1) + alignment - 1) payload/padding bytes,
+plus all actual records/control.  Alignment is a positive power of two at
+least max_align_t; request_bytes cannot exceed PTRDIFF_MAX.  Counts are
+CUMULATIVE, not just simultaneous live counts.  Realloc really allocates a
+new record/payload before copying and releasing the original; refusal leaves
+the original live and unchanged.  No physical record or block is reused.
+Zero-size allocation uses one actual byte and one record.  There is no heap,
+mmap, loader or unpriced bookkeeping fallback inside the allocator.
+
+A separate process-lifetime application_state contains 128 immutable slot
+identities/ranges plus serial, refusal/uncertainty counters and explicit
+64-byte alignment/padding.  Even a returned arena consumes its slot forever.
+The current LP64 field layout yields the following SOURCE-derived sizes:
+
+  configuration                  40 bytes, alignment 8
+  application receipt           168 bytes, alignment 8
+  application control           216 bytes, alignment 8
+  allocation record              48 bytes, alignment 8
+  attachment record             112 bytes, alignment 8
+  process slot                   48 bytes, alignment 8
+  process object               6208 bytes, alignment 64 (padding included)
+  pthread owner application field 8 bytes
+  pthread descriptor app field    8 bytes before enclosing TLS/TCB padding
+
+These are not compiler/ELF measurements.  malloc_bounded_layout_np reports the
+compiled sizeof/alignment, complete pthread control and pthread descriptor
+sizes.  The previous metadata control was 544 bytes; the source adds one
+pointer (expected 552 before compiler confirmation).  The prior measured
+thread descriptor was 2432 bytes; its new total and resulting static TLS/TCB
+alignment increment must be measured with the actual compiler.  New ELF .bss
+placement/alignment, fixed registry cost, complete TLS geometry and the final
+loaded libc/loader footprint must enter the real Source/QEMU process floor
+before any profile could accept.  No per-role scalar allowance pays these
+process globals, serials, locks or TLS increments implicitly.
+
+Explicit no-fail invocation boundary
+-----------------------------------
+
+Three additional GLIBC_2.39 symbols, malloc_bounded_failstop_begin_np,
+malloc_bounded_failstop_end_np and malloc_bounded_failstop_query_np, add an
+explicit selected failure scope.  The existing v1 configuration, layout and
+receipt structures do not grow.  The actual attachment record includes a
+new 80-byte scope journal (source-derived LP64 geometry); its larger compiled
+size must be queried before backing admission.  There is no additional TLS
+pointer, process registry, allocator or diagnostic heap.  Fresh ELF/code/page
+and all actual object geometry still require measurement of this revision.
+
+Begin authenticates live child membership before dereference, its expected
+incarnation and the exact CURRENT attachment.  It takes the shared gate once,
+excludes all retained/fixed spans from the output and returns a monotonically
+increasing per-attachment nonce.  UINT64_MAX refuses before wrap.  Scopes do
+not nest.  End requires the same current attachment and exact active nonce;
+a wrong or stale end cannot close a later scope.  An active scope refuses
+attachment detach.  Query is independently canonical and includes successful
+scope identity, active/ended/abandoned facts, first error, most recent begin
+and end statuses, and the nominated end nonce.  Begin/end attempt counters
+saturate as diagnostic lower bounds.  A busy gate returns EBUSY before owner
+authentication, so the caller must retain that actual attempted/returned
+operation separately; it cannot be added to an unauthenticated child journal.
+
+Nonce output follows the full existing external-span rule.  A local variable
+on a registered supplied stack is deliberately ineligible, even in that
+worker.  The containing caller must supply independently retained output
+control outside every registered stack, metadata/application arena and fixed
+container, keeping it alive through the invocation and real terminal tail.
+The worker cannot manufacture that control from application payload credit.
+The terminal fixture uses Main-retained control and a release/acquire ready
+handshake after the actual create operation returns, so a creator's receipt
+publication cannot accidentally become the intended begin-failure cause.
+
+While selected, a raw libc allocation refusal issues one internal Linux
+exit_group(127) process-termination syscall, without logging, stdio, allocation,
+callbacks, exit handlers or allocator locks.  If that syscall returns, an
+explicit compiler trap follows; there is no userspace retry loop.  Unlike
+stock _exit, this boundary does not retry a rejected termination syscall.
+The runtime must permit process termination and must not convert the terminal
+trap into continued application execution.  Kernel scheduling/termination
+latency is not a userspace nonwaiting cleanup guarantee.  This includes
+exhausted bounds, a busy allocation gate,
+libc calloc/pvalloc arithmetic overflow and refused raw realloc.  Hidden libc
+allocation entry points use the same routing.  The checked malloc_bounded
+allocation/reallocation APIs still RETURN their actual status and preserve
+failed outputs/old allocations.  Void-free uncertainty remains independent
+sticky debt; this mode does not invent a successful release or backing post.
+Unattached behavior and allocation-returning behavior outside the explicit
+scope remain unchanged.  Process termination is a FAILED invocation, not
+successful execution, cleanup or any reusable storage/debit return.
+
+The real GLib 2.82.4 g_malloc/g_malloc0/g_realloc functions call libc and enter
+g_error after a NULL allocation result.  Their g_try_* counterparts use the
+same libc symbols and return NULL.  libc cannot infer which contract is in
+effect from attachment, a return address or an allocator symbol.  The caller
+must select the scope only around an audited no-fail invocation; try calls
+must stay outside it.  Its entire synchronous closure, including signals and
+callbacks, observes that selected policy.  A mixed callback/handler that needs
+recoverable NULL makes that proposed invocation ineligible until its actual
+producer supplies a compatible boundary.  No call-site adoption is provided.
+
+GLib g_malloc_n/g_malloc0_n/g_realloc_n perform multiplication diagnostics
+BEFORE libc; g_aligned_alloc also diagnoses alignment and overflow itself.
+Those effects are NOT intercepted by this libc scope.  A complete caller
+needs genuinely checked finite arguments at those producers or a separately
+implemented/reviewed GLib failure producer.  A test of ordinary valid arguments
+cannot establish that closure.  No GLib feature or try behavior is disabled.
+
+If a bound thread exits with a selected invocation still open, only the real
+external pthread terminal cut returns its attachment after kernel join and
+TLS/TSD/allocator tail completion.  It also marks the scope abandoned and the
+application slot uncertain.  Factual terminal_threads/attachment.returned and
+known allocation releases are preserved, but application retirement refuses
+EPROTO, even when attached and live counts are zero.  This cannot be repaired
+by a copied end receipt, a new attachment, or CPU/notifier departure.  The
+caller retains backing and process debt through reap.  No-birth attachments
+cannot have entered a current-thread scope, so ordinary factual no-birth
+retirement remains unaffected.
+
+Finite source controls, not yet executed
+---------------------------------------
+
+The static component now includes actual exhausted malloc, held-gate malloc
+and overflowing calloc subprocesses.  Each must exit 127 without output;
+returning NULL instead reaches a distinct 126 exit.  Additional controls
+exercise nested refusal, peer output exclusion, unchanged failed checked
+allocation output, stale scope end, held-gate end, sticky first error alongside
+known successful end, ordinary NULL behavior after end, and actual bound
+thread TLS/TSD tail with an intentionally abandoned scope.  The latter must
+preserve true pthread join/TLS return and application pin return, then refuse
+application retirement with zero live allocations and sticky uncertainty.
+
+The external configured GLib DSO component adds real g_malloc, g_malloc0,
+g_realloc and g_strdup_printf/__vasprintf_chk failure subprocesses.  The actual
+DSO's default logger and structured writer are installed before attachment;
+entering either exits distinctly with 126.  Returning from the no-fail call
+exits 125.  Only allocation-free exit 127 passes.  g_try_malloc outside the
+scope must still return NULL.  The selected cohort excludes pre-libc GLib
+overflow/argument errors and mixed try callbacks.  None of these new controls
+has run; no old installed runtime can qualify the new symbols or record size.
+
+Remaining real producer and containing-owner closure
+----------------------------------------------------
+
+The concrete caller split remains (a) process/context-retained objects and
+published global cache roots, and (b) translation/callback workspace with its
+own actual final reference.  Each needs separately admitted backing under the
+same retained parent, exact cross-thread origin, and producer memberships saved
+before publication or fallible construction.  Allocation ownership selects a
+physical release target; it does not authorize a callback or close a producer.
+
+A finite native cohort must close new Main/RR/finalizer/callback publication,
+finish every already admitted invocation, drain each admitted RCU producer's
+actual queue epoch, and retain all TCG/context/hot-fork/global cache references
+through their real last use.  A single queue marker cannot close another
+producer, and ordinary kernel join cannot return published context custody.
+Only then can all scoped attachments end, bound attachments complete actual
+TLS/TSD tails, every live allocation release, and all independent negative or
+uncertain posts be assessed.  No external callback runs under a libc gate.
+
+The finite allocator's zero counts provide a necessary storage observation,
+not that containing-owner proof.  The present libc neither creates those
+native producer memberships nor implements their stop/drain adapters.  It
+continues to retain all 128 lifetime slots and admitted VA extents.  Complete
+local reuse would additionally require an exclusive genuine final-lifetime
+owner and a nonwaiting reader reclamation protocol before replacing range
+publication; neither exists here.  Full profile acceptance therefore remains
+unavailable.  Process-reap-only quarantine is still an explicit physical debt,
+never silently settled by this failure scope or a successful libc retirement.
+
+Address quarantine is a distinct retained resource
+-----------------------------------------------
+
+Every admitted application extent is permanently tombstoned in this process.
+Init rejects overlap with every old application extent, including returned
+ones.  registry_used/capacity/slot and address_quarantined remain factual.
+registration_removed means canonical live membership was removed; it does
+NOT rearm the global slot or make the address reusable.  Exhaustion is ENOSPC
+before writes, with no wrap, eviction or alternate registry.
+
+libc_custody_returned relinquishes libc's access/custody only.  It does not unmap,
+free, madvise or perform the containing owner's actual return.  The caller
+must retain the virtual-address reservation through actual process reap.
+It must not unmap/remap, reinitialize, lend the extent to an ordinary allocator,
+or reuse the numeric address for another object in this process.  A containing
+owner that cannot retain this VA/quarantine debt cannot admit this component.
+Physical-page return, VA debt and independent return posts remain separate
+caller facts.  Thus this component does not provide whole-child rearm/reuse.
+A raw free lacks an allocation generation; its exactly-once last-reference
+contract remains required even with process-lifetime quarantine.  Checked calls
+add the expected canonical child incarnation and allocation id.
+
+The raw routing classifier acquires each immutable process slot extent.  Init
+publishes its immutable base and initial active state before the release
+publication of that extent.  Neither base nor extent is replaced or cleared.
+Successful retirement clears active slot.bytes and the canonical owner under
+the gate, retaining the permanent numeric classifier.  A later raw free,
+realloc or malloc_usable_size on that known retired extent refuses without
+reading returned arena metadata or entering ordinary ptmalloc.  Realloc returns
+NULL/ESTALE; usable-size returns zero; void free retains process uncertainty
+and a refusal lower bound.  Retired-range, refusal and uncertain-slot aggregates
+remain queryable through the layout API, which uses process storage only.
+They are not per-child return posts or proof that raw pointers are valid.
+
+Every such post-lifetime raw access remains invalid C use.  Negative routing
+does not authenticate pointer generations, extend a lifetime, rearm a slot,
+return a reservation, or create storage/operation authority.  Checked calls on
+the retired canonical control return ESRCH before control dereference.  The
+containing owner must still close every producer BEFORE custody retirement.
+
+Potential final-lifetime reuse contract, not implemented
+-------------------------------------------------------
+
+The permanent reservation is a restriction of this component, not a claim
+that an ordinary valid C allocation requires indefinite stale-pointer safety.
+A future reusable child could end its lifetime under an exclusive containing
+owner after every actual producer has closed, every callback/finalizer and
+RCU/cache/context last use has completed, every scoped/bound attachment has
+returned, every physical allocation has returned and all independent posts
+are known.  That owner would retain the original backing and canonical child
+through this final cut, authenticate expected child/allocation/attachment
+incarnations while live, and serialize retirement against every participant.
+It would then perform and record its real physical backing/VA/debit return
+before admitting another child, using a fresh nonwrapping incarnation.
+
+After such a genuine final lifetime, using a stale raw pointer is invalid C
+use; raw free cannot prove a pointer generation.  This does not justify
+reusing blocks during an active child, weakening checked active-child ABA
+checks, inferring producer closure from zero live counts, or treating a
+copied receipt as the containing owner's authority.  A real reusable design
+also needs a nonwaiting publication/reclamation protocol for slot/range
+classification readers before replacing process registry entries.  No such
+protocol, exclusive parent proof, final-last-use integration or reusable
+retirement ABI is implemented here.  Current tombstones and VA debt remain
+mandatory and the 128-admission cumulative limit remains unchanged.
+
+Canonical membership and nonwaiting cuts
+--------------------------------------
+
+The shared actual pthread registration gate serializes both registries,
+reciprocal span validation and publication.  Every acquisition is a single
+atomic exchange, never a retry/spin/futex.  Lookup compares the nominated
+control against finite canonical entries before dereference, then authenticates
+its nonwrapping incarnation.  Checked record lookup authenticates the exact
+pointer/id without dereferencing a nominated pointer or invented prefix.
+
+All new checked writable outputs exclude every retained metadata arena/stack,
+all application extents including quarantine, the entire aligned application
+process object, and the metadata registry/serial/shared lock.  Registration
+and supplied-stack reservation enforce reciprocal exclusions before writes.
+Libc cannot enumerate the native caller's foreign control extents: the caller
+must retain and exclude those through its actual parent/child protocol too.
+Ordinary C allocator output pointers retain their ordinary writable-memory
+contract; these routines are not a process memory-isolation boundary.
+
+Range classification while the gate is busy reads only process-lifetime
+atomics.  Slots/ranges are not replaced.  It never reads possibly returned
+arena metadata.  A busy checked operation returns EBUSY before effect.  Void
+raw free cannot communicate a no-effect status: on gate contention it records
+sticky uncertainty/quarantine and retains the live record.  Even a later
+known checked release and equal allocated/released counts cannot erase that
+uncertainty or permit terminal return.  Malformed/duplicate/foreign frees
+retain sticky debt; no foreign free is silently treated as successful return.
+The refusal counter is a saturated monotonic lower bound: one strong CAS,
+with competing increments permitted to coalesce, no retry and no wrap.
+
+Attachments and actual TLS/TSD terminal return
+--------------------------------------------
+
+Scoped attach returns a cumulative attachment nonce; detach requires that exact
+current nonce and child incarnation.  A stale same-child detach cannot close a
+later scope.  Nested attachment, replacement and accidental cross-parent
+replacement refuse.  Ordinary pthread_create does not inherit attachments.
+The native caller may attach a genuinely admitted Main preparation or a typed
+RCU/callback producer; an allocation-owned pointer grants storage return target,
+not callback/operation permission.  Heterogeneous ordinary RCU work stays on its
+ordinary route; callbacks execute outside allocator gates.
+
+bind authenticates both application and READY pthread controls/incarnations
+and saves a canonical application attachment before any thread-creation effect.
+Its nonce and per-attachment query bind the exact actual pthread owner.  The
+child gets this pin before entry, including setup failure/never-entered birth.
+It cannot explicitly detach a thread-bound pin.  The pin remains through exit
+notifiers, __call_tls_dtors, __nptl_deallocate_tsd, __libc_thread_freeres and the
+actual kernel exit.  Only successful genuine pthread_bounded_tryretire_np,
+after known no-birth or actual kernel join plus list/TLS/metadata closure,
+marks the pin returned.  User return, notifier/registry departure, stopped CPU,
+clear-tid alone, copied receipt or child self-report cannot return that pin.
+Uncertain pthread cleanup retains it.  Escaping a scoped attachment without
+detach also retains it; no destructor tries to manufacture missing lifetime.
+
+All five bounded metadata producers still use their original metadata owner:
+DTV, dynamic TLS, second-level TSD, TLS destructor nodes and priority bookkeeping.
+Scoped ordinary Main attachment does not silently fund missing metadata:
+ordinary pthread construction, missing second-level TSD/TPP and new TLS dtor
+nodes refuse ENOTSUP.  Lazy Main DTV growth/dynamic TLS lacks a recoverable ABI
+and takes the loader's truthful fatal path before heap allocation.  It may not
+be priced as application storage.  Ordinary unattached paths stay unchanged.
+Bounded pthread loader/destructor/control allocation remains metadata-local.
+The original strict resolver/RPC/error/ordinary foreign-free refusals remain;
+application storage does not waive those execution-profile restrictions.
+
+History and non-claims
+----------------------
+
+Canonical per-allocation queries preserve actual address, id, size/alignment,
+creator attachment, release attempts and known release; per-attachment queries
+preserve exact pthread owner/incarnation and actual return.  Receipts retain
+first_error separately from live/returned facts, refusal lower bounds and sticky
+invalid/uncertain release debt.  Successful later physical facts do not erase
+negative history.  The caller retains independent post attempted/returned/status
+and consumed facts itself; libc does not fabricate those posts.
+
+Tryretire requires zero actual live allocations and attachments plus no invalid
+or uncertain release debt.  It returns only libc arena custody and retains
+process slot/VA quarantine.  Process/context TCG objects, PageDesc/radix/qht/QTree,
+plugin/random state, finalizers and RCU last use remain the caller's actual
+containing-owner obligations.  Neither kernel join nor a zero allocator live
+count establishes producer closure or global cache return.
+
+Creation, loader lookup, callback execution, destructor code and stop/drain
+progress can wait.  Only allocator/registry cuts and terminal polling are
+nonwaiting; there is no whole-RR execution/exit nonwaiting claim.  Arbitrary
+application lifetime allocations, callback volume, GLib no-fail exhaustion,
+feature-dependent allocator paths and process quiescence are not universally
+bounded by this component.  GLib g_malloc/g_new failure remains fatal by its
+real API, never accepted execution or an excuse to disable supported features.
+
+Concrete controls: source only, not results
+-----------------------------------------
+
+tst-malloc-bounded is an internal STATIC libc test so it can hold the actual
+shared gate.  -fno-builtin preserves real allocation calls.  It exercises:
+  real cumulative exhaustion with no fallback; alignment and calloc overflow;
+  realloc failure preserving old bytes, real move/copy/old release;
+  scoped pin nonce/nesting/stale detach; Main-created storage freed by another
+  ordinary pthread; actual checked gate refusal and later factual return;
+  duplicate/interior/raw-busy release debt, including known releases with
+  equal counters but sticky quarantine; peer-live outputs preserving bytes;
+  canonical pointer refusal, tombstoned-address refusal before PROT_NONE write;
+  invalid raw free/realloc/usable on an actually PROT_NONE retired arena, with
+  no ordinary allocator fallback, queryable process debt and stale-control ESRCH;
+  guarded supplied-stack birth, TLS dtor plus second-level TSD allocation
+  remaining metadata-local while real application objects return; held TSD
+  destructor, actual kernel exit with application pin still retained, then
+  matching provider retirement; known no-birth pin return; and all 128 actual
+  process slots exhausted without clearing old reservations or touching the
+  first refused arena/outputs.
+
+The external tst-malloc-bounded-glib.c uses actual configured public GLib
+headers/DSOs, not copied implementations or allocator shims.  It calls real
+DSO g_malloc/g_malloc0/g_realloc/g_free, growing GArray/GHashTable, seeded GRand
+copy/free, g_strdup_printf and actual __vasprintf_chk with 10000-byte growth.
+Every canonical produced allocation is inspected for real arena custody,
+producer pin and known return before retirement.  It must compile with
+-D_GNU_SOURCE -UNDEBUG -fno-builtin against the newly built public libc header,
+matching complete startup/static support, loader and libc, plus the reviewed
+GLib-2.82.4 development include/glib-2.0 and lib/glib-2.0/include directories.
+The actual DSO path, full ELF binding/dependency closure, input/output hashes
+and raw exit/diagnostic must be retained when executed.  It is deliberately
+not a default glibc build dependency.  The older installed dae19 runtime lacks
+these symbols and cannot qualify this source.
+
+Before execution, independently review/freeze these sources, allocate one
+compiler explicitly, make a private regular source/build copy, retain the
+2-GiB per-process cap and standard source-built dependencies, and preserve all
+first outcomes.  No compiler, new libc installation or runtime control has
+been run for this revision.  Follow-up controls must include bound dynamic
+TLS/DTV growth, born-never-entered setup failure with app binding, actual GLib
+TSD tail, intentional no-fail GLib exhaustion/fatal outcome, signal reentry,
+negative independent caller posts and actual complete loaded-runtime geometry.
+Existing strict and ordinary pthread tests must be rerun; old PASS is not new
+qualification.  Missing producers, resource lanes or last-use closure refuse.
+
+Static archive dependency boundary
+
+The first full build of revision 908402f stopped at the required static
+support/test-run-command link: ordinary malloc pulled the application router,
+then the metadata registry object, then bounded pthread creation and its
+cancellation unwinder.  libgcc's _Unwind_Resume conflicted with the intentional
+static support stubs for programs which do not use cancellation.  This was a
+real link failure, not a memory limit or an accepted test exception.
+
+Bounded creation now occupies a separate archive member.  Registry, allocation,
+attachment and terminal-owner operations retain their actual shared helpers;
+only a creation reference pulls pthread creation and cancellation dependencies.
+Public symbols, metadata/application routing and the static support stubs stay
+unchanged.  Qualification requires a fresh full build including the original
+support/test-run-command link, in addition to the finite controls above.
+
+Canonical complete process inventory
+
+The additive malloc-bounded-process.h interface leaves the original v1 layout
+and process_bytes field unchanged.  That field still measures the application
+registry alone.  malloc_bounded_process_np directly returns this loaded
+provider's actual immutable process owner.  Current authenticates that exact
+singleton address before reading nominated memory, checks the actual getpid
+observation against the caller's expected process, then takes the shared gate
+once and validates its entire output before writing anything.
+
+The fixed inventory contains three complete compiled objects: application
+registry (all permanent classifiers, counters, serial and activation state),
+shared pthread registry (all128 pointers, serial, shared gate and explicit
+container padding), and the immutable process identity/control itself.  There
+is no separate hidden descriptor table.  The snapshot's inline inventory is
+caller storage.  Total object bytes use checked addition; each actual base,
+sizeof and alignment is reported, rather than copying the historical1056-byte
+metadata object observation.  The full objects, including container padding
+and readonly identity, are excluded by reciprocal admission and all checked
+writable-output cuts.  No allocator or callback runs under these cuts.
+
+This inventory does not allocate/fund process storage.  The genuine containing
+owner must retain the direct singleton and independently authenticated native
+process-generation/loaded-libc binding, account for these actual objects once
+under the same actual resource parent, and preserve that account until process
+reap.  Copied inventory fields, PID, slot counts and owner addresses grant no
+semantic operation, storage child, debit/refund or hot-fork adoption.  Object
+spans can share ELF pages with other libc data; matching ELF mapping/page/TLS
+floors remain separately required.  There is no process-owner retirement or
+rearm API, no alias to an unrelated parent's account, and no generation proof
+for a stale address reused outside the valid canonical process lifetime.
+
+After fork the inherited singleton address is numerically equal, but Current
+with the parent's saved PID refuses ESTALE without publishing output.  A
+query using the child's actual PID only observes that child; it cannot
+reconcile inherited physical backing, reservations, pins or Source debits.
+Actual hot-fork adoption and complete parent/process custody remain with the
+caller's independently reviewed lifetime protocol.  All supported execution
+features and ordinary unattached allocation behavior remain present.
+
+New finite source controls check direct canonical identity, exact actual
+container membership/geometry, complete-object/padding/readonly output alias
+refusal with bytes preserved, peer-live application alias refusal, held real
+gate EBUSY with unchanged output, nominated invalid owner before dereference,
+and real fork preserving numeric singleton while rejecting parent identity.
+These controls are unexecuted until a newly allocated compiler lane qualifies
+this exact revision.  Prior39425 component qualification is preserved and does
+not establish this changed global layout or its installed public ABI.  No
+Source issuance or complete runtime profile is admitted by source review.
